prompt:
Use Three.js to implement a duck pond water-wave physics simulation. Place 10 ducks on the water surface, implement a height-field water-ripple simulation, make the ducks move with the water surface, and support wind effects.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { GLTFLoader } from 'three/addons/loaders/GLTFLoader.js';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Model Loading]
(1) Use GLTFLoader to load assets/Duck.glb;
(2) After loading is complete, clone 10 duck instances and distribute them randomly across the water surface (within an area of approximately 20x20);
(3) After loading is complete, set window.__3D_STATE__.modelLoaded = true;

[Water-Surface Simulation]
(4) Create a PlaneGeometry water surface (with at least 100x100 subdivisions) using a translucent blue material (MeshPhongMaterial, color: 0x2288cc, transparent: true, opacity: 0.7);
(5) Implement a height-field water-wave simulation: maintain a two-dimensional array that records the height of each vertex, and update it using the wave equation (current height = 2*current - previous frame + damping * Laplacian-operator neighborhood average);
(6) On every frame, apply the height array to the Y coordinates of the water geometry's vertices, and call geometry.attributes.position.needsUpdate = true;
(7) Recompute the normals using geometry.computeVertexNormals() so that lighting reflections are correct;

[Water-Surface Interaction]
(8) Use Raycaster to detect the position of mouse clicks on the water surface;
(9) When the water surface is clicked, create a ripple at that position (set the height-field values near the clicked position to a relatively large positive value);
(10) Each time a ripple is created, increment window.__3D_STATE__.rippleCount++;

[Duck Buoyancy]
(11) On every frame, calculate the water-surface height at each duck's position by interpolating its position on the water-surface grid;
(12) Set each duck's Y coordinate to that water-surface height so that the duck moves up and down with the waves;
(13) Optional: slightly tilt each duck according to the local normal of the water surface;

[Wind System]
(14) Add a wind-force slider (id="windSlider", range 0-10, step 0.5, default 0) representing wind speed in m/s;
(15) Wind effects:
  a) Height-field ripples propagate faster along the wind direction (the +X direction by default);
  b) The ducks are pushed by the wind and slowly drift along the wind direction (drift speed is proportional to wind speed);
  c) The ducks stop drifting when they reach the boundary;
(16) Update window.__3D_STATE__.windSpeed to the current wind-speed value;

[HUD Display]
(17) Display the following in the HUD panel in the upper-left corner:
  - Average water-surface height at the ducks' positions (id="avgWaterHeight");
  - Average duck velocity (id="avgDuckVelocity", calculated based on position changes);
  - Ripple count (id="rippleCount");
  - Current wind speed (id="windDisplay");
  - Model-loading status (id="loadStatus");

[OrbitControls]
(18) Use OrbitControls to provide top-down/orbit viewing, with an initial viewing angle slightly from above (approximately 45 degrees);

[Visual Effects]
(19) Use a light-blue sky color (0x87ceeb) for the scene background;
(20) Add a DirectionalLight (simulating the sun) and an AmbientLight;
(21) Optional: add a sand-colored plane beneath the water surface as the pond bottom;

[State Exposure (Required)]
Update window.__3D_STATE__ on every frame:
  - modelLoaded: boolean (whether the model has finished loading)
  - duckCount: number (number of ducks, which should be 10)
  - avgWaterHeight: number (average water-surface height at the ducks' positions)
  - avgDuckVelocity: number (average duck drift speed)
  - rippleCount: number (cumulative number of ripples)
  - windSpeed: number (current wind speed, 0-10)
  - duckPositions: Array<{x,y,z}> (positions of the 10 ducks)

Output a single HTML file. Place this file in the same directory as the assets/ folder, and run it through a local HTTP server.
